= Real-World Active Directory Case Studies: GOAD and Community Benchmarks <ch:case_study>


== From Synthetic Topologies to Enterprise Deployments

While controlled synthetic environments provide the mathematical foundation necessary to train neural architectures and isolate experimental variables, enterprise cybersecurity requires models to generalize seamlessly to real, messy, production-grade operating systems. Real-world Active Directory deployments exhibit structural idiosyncrasies that rarely appear in idealized synthetic generators:

+ Multi-forest trust topologies with complex Kerberos referral tickets and SID filtering boundaries.
+ Highly nested security group hierarchies created over decades of administrative churn.
+ Legacy access control entries (ACEs) inheriting permissions across deep Organizational Unit (OU) trees.
+ Partial or noisy directory collections caused by network timeouts, firewall packet filtering, and endpoint sensor coverage gaps.



To establish whether CertGraph generalizes beyond synthetic graphs to real Windows enterprise infrastructures, we deployed our evaluation framework against the *Game of Active Directory (GOAD)* @garla2020game testbed, followed by multi-week temporal validation and external community-collected enterprise datasets.

== The Game of Active Directory (GOAD) Multi-Forest Testbed

GOAD is an acclaimed, widely recognized vulnerable enterprise research environment deployed on dedicated virtualization hardware. The infrastructure mirrors the complex identity architecture of a multi-national conglomerate spanning five dedicated virtual machines running licensed Windows Server 2016, Windows Server 2019, and Windows 10 Enterprise operating systems:


+ *`sevenkingdoms.local` (Parent Root Domain):* Hosted on Domain Controller `kingslanding.sevenkingdoms.local` (Windows Server 2019, DC01). Manages 16 human user accounts, 59 global and universal security groups, Enterprise Certificate Services infrastructure, and authoritative Tier-0 administrative roles (`Enterprise Admins`, `Schema Admins`).
+ *`north.sevenkingdoms.local` (Child Domain):* Operates in a bidirectional transitive parent-child domain trust with the parent domain. Hosted across two dedicated systems: Primary Domain Controller `winterfell.north.sevenkingdoms.local` (Windows Server 2019, DC02) and secondary enterprise server `castelblack.north.sevenkingdoms.local` (Windows Server 2016, SRV02). Manages 5 regional user accounts and 47 security groups.
+ *`essos.local` (External Forest):* An independent corporate forest connected to `sevenkingdoms.local` via a one-way, non-transitive external forest trust with selective authentication and SID filtering. Hosted on Domain Controller `meereen.essos.local` (Windows Server 2019, DC03) and enterprise member server `braavos.essos.local` (Windows Server 2016, SRV03) hosting IIS web services and the Enterprise CA. Manages 14 user accounts and 60 security groups.



As illustrated in @fig:goad_topology, the multi-forest architecture features interconnected parent, child, and external domains. Of critical operational relevance to this thesis, the Enterprise Certificate Authority (`ADCS - ESSOS-CA`) is hosted on member server `braavos` (`SRV03`), representing an authentic enterprise PKI target accessible via both LDAP enrollment and cross-domain authentication links.

