== Temporal Validation Across Independent Collection Dates

A major challenge in machine learning evaluations is sensitivity to temporal environmental drift and collection artifacts. In operational enterprise environments, Active Directory graphs mutate continuously as users log on, temporary sessions expire, and security groups are modified.

To establish whether our models maintain stable representations over time, we conducted three independent data collections against the running GOAD virtual machines across distinct calendar dates:

+ *Collection Baseline (T1):* June 15, 2026.
+ *Follow-up Snapshot (T2):* July 2, 2026 (17 days later, following intermediate red-team simulation exercises).
+ *Validation Snapshot (T3):* August 10, 2026 (56 days later, following system patching and host reboots).



In our temporal testbed, 7 representative certificate template configurations were deployed in each of the 3 domains (`sevenkingdoms.local`, `north.sevenkingdoms.local`, and `essos.local`), representing 21 distinct template evaluations per snapshot date. Evaluated across the 3 independent collection dates, this generated $21 times 3 = 63$ temporal evaluation instances:

+ `sevenkingdoms.local`: $7 / 7$ test instances correctly classified across all 3 dates ($21 / 21$, $100.0%$).
+ `north.sevenkingdoms.local`: $7 / 7$ test instances correctly classified across all 3 dates ($21 / 21$, $100.0%$).
+ `essos.local`: $7 / 7$ test instances correctly classified across all 3 dates ($21 / 21$, $100.0%$).
+ *Aggregate Temporal Accuracy (CertGraph-Hybrid):* $bold(63 / 63)$ test instances correctly classified ($bold(100.0%)$, 95% Wilson CI: $[0.942, 1.000]$).


This multi-week temporal validation confirms that CertGraph-Hybrid's relational representations and path verification are invariant to transient session noise and background administrative churn.

== External Community Benchmark Validation

To eliminate dataset collection bias and verify model robustness on telemetry collected outside our local virtualization lab, we evaluated CertGraph and CertGraph-Hybrid against an independent open-source enterprise dataset from the `m4lwhere/Bloodhound-CE-Sample-Data` repository. This repository contains raw SharpHound telemetry collected from an external, multi-domain GOADv2 installation managed by independent security researchers, providing an exact-topology replication study with fully independent collection artifacts.

